Z||000005||KissMySSFiero||10-24-2001||01:10 PM||||Dont be fooled by what you hear on commercials.  unless your car calls for the higher octane you are just throwing money away.  you do need high octane fuel in high compression engines.  unless you engine has ingition knock run the cheapest you can get.  <P>------------------<BR>JOEJOE Aol:SSFiero <BR>88FORMULA<BR>       <A HREF="mailto:SSFiero@Aol.com">SSFiero@Aol.com</A>        <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FORUMSIG1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||William Federle||10-24-2001||01:36 PM||||I have a 1984 SE (w/iron duke). I used regular, but it pinged when going up the hill between Milwaukee and Fond du Lac WI.<P>Then I tried mixing 1/2 tank of regular with 1/2 tank of 93. Less pinging, but it was still there. <P>Now I just use 93. No pinging. I get about 30 MPG and it's about 1.50/gal right now.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Master_Sushi||10-24-2001||01:39 PM||||I allways use premium. I do notice a difference in my car if I run cheap gas. Same thing with the other cars I drive, I can tell if someone put in regular.||||reg||1
Z||000008||isthiswhereiputausername?||10-24-2001||01:50 PM||||I use the flamable stuff  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>actually I use regular gas in our cars<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Oreif||10-24-2001||02:02 PM||||I use Premium. The advantage to premium (91-93 octane) is 2 reasons. First reason is the "flash" point is higher which is why it causes less pinging (pre-dentonation). The second reason is higher octane causes less carbon soot. So the valves will stay cleaner, O2 sensors and Catalytic converters stay cleaner and it helps the car put out lower emissions. The reason switching from 87 to 93 may feel different is it will clean out some of the carbon. Cleaner valves also means better flow hence better mileage. It will not give you "extra" horsepower but will restore what may be lost due to carbon build-up.<P>On a new engine I doubt you could tell the difference what gas is used. Both economy and performace would most likely be the same. It's just as the engine gets more miles on it does it make a slight difference.<P> I also believe that some cars get "use" to the gas that is used. Like 72vinman states, when he changed fuel grades it acted worse. I use only Shell SU 2000 and my car runs great, once I put in some Amoco Ultimate and the car ran worse. I have no idea why but I switched back to Shell and everything was fine. I doubt there is that much different between brands and/or grades but changing brands or grades from what the car normally ran for many years seems to affect the cars.<P><BR>------------------<BR>             <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">           <BR><FONT SIZE="1"><B><I>Happiness isn't around the corner...<BR>Happiness IS the corner.</I></B></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 10-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||Old Lar||10-24-2001||02:48 PM||||Been using 87 Octane in both GTs. Noooo problems. Never noticed any difference when using higher octane. Actually, I did, my mpg decreased.<P>Even used some Sunoco 86 octane and the car still ran fine.<P>Mostly all highway driving so cruising at 70-75 most of the time.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Voytek||10-24-2001||02:53 PM||||I've seen this question a few times before and it absolutely AMAZES me that there are some people who say that premium gas is the same (or no better) than regular gas.<P>First, ask yourself this question: why does premium gas cost more?  The answer is obvious: it IS better gas.<P>Oreif explained it well, but here is a copy of an article from a website called How Stuff Works (this was the question of the day):<P>Question <BR>What does octane mean? <P>Answer<BR>If you've read the HSW article entitled How Car Engines Work, you know that almost all cars use 4-stroke gasoline engines. One of the strokes is the compression stroke, where the engine compresses a cylinder-full of air and gas into a much smaller volume before igniting it with a spark plug. The amount of compression is called the compression ratio of the engine. A typical engine might have a compression ratio of 8-to-1. See the How Car Engines Work article for details. <BR>The octane rating of gasoline tells you the amount that the fuel can be compressed before it spontaneously ignites. When gas ignites by compression rather than because of the spark from the spark plug, it causes knocking in the engine. Knocking can damage an engine, so it is not something you want have happening. Lower octane gas (like "regular" 87 octane gasoline) can handle the least amount of compression before igniting. <P>The compression ratio of your engine determines the octane rating of the gas you must use in the car. One way to increase the horsepower of an engine of a given displacement is to increase its compression ratio. So a "high performance engine" has a higher compression ratio and requires higher octane fuel. The advantage of a high compression ratio is that it gives your engine a higher horsepower rating for a given engine weight -- that is what makes the engine "high performance". The disadvantage is that the gasoline for your engine costs more. <P>The name "octane" comes from the following fact. When you take crude oil and "crack" it in a refinery, you end up getting hydrocarbon chains of different lengths. These different chain lengths can then be separated from each other and blended to form different fuels. For example, you may have heard of methane, propane and butane. All three of them are hydrocarbons. Methane has just a single carbon atom. Propane has three carbon atoms chained together. Butane has four carbon atoms chained together. Pentane has 5, hexane has 6, heptane has 7 and octane has 8 carbons chained together. <P>It turns out that heptane handles compression very poorly. Compress it just a little and it ignites spontaneously. Octane handles compression very well -- you can compress it a lot and nothing happens. 87 octane gasoline is gasoline that contains 87% octane and 13% heptane (or some other combination of fuels that has the same performance of the 87/13 combination of octane/heptane). It spontaneously ignites at a given compression level, and can only be used in engines that do not exceed that compression ratio. <P>During WWI, it was discovered that you can add a chemical called tetraethyl lead to gasoline and significantly improve its octane rating. Cheaper grades of gasoline could be made usable by adding this chemical. This led to the widespread use of "ethyl" or "leaded" gasoline. Unfortunately, the side effects of adding lead to gasoline are: 1) the fact that lead clogs a catalytic converter and renders it inoperable within minutes, and 2) the fact that the earth became covered in a thin layer of lead, and lead is toxic to many living things (including humans). When lead was banned, gasoline got more expensive because refineries could not boost the octane ratings of cheaper grades any more. Airplanes are still allowed to use leaded gasoline, and octane ratings of 115 are commonly used in super-high-performance piston airplane engines (jets burn kerosene, by the way).<P> <BR>Any questions?<P>Oh, one more thing.  My Fiero ALWAYS runs better (and a bit faster) on high octane gas.<p>[This message has been edited by Voytek (edited 10-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||Maniak84||10-24-2001||02:59 PM||||Yeah, I have a question. What's the best type of gas for huffing?<P>---------------------------------------<P>Can i put nitrus in my 1984 feiro 2m4?it's a 4cilinder||||reg||1
Z||000013||72vinman||10-24-2001||03:00 PM||||Orief, do you suggest I continue using the high octane for a little while longer to see if it clears things up. Or maybe even adding a injector cleaner with it as well?? I never thought about it cleaning through the old carbon.||||reg||1
Z||000014||falconhulk||10-24-2001||03:09 PM||||regular gas is good for a engine designed for it.  A high compression engine, timing modified engine (i.e. advanced OR retarded) , super or tubrocharged.  It is also good to use in older engines.  I use premium in mine.  If your car does not have a performance engine or any modifications that require it regular is fine.  In a fiero with a non rebuilt original engine I would use high octane because of the detergents it contains.  These detergents are what people think make it better gas.  A new v8 engine with standard compression , even in the camaro or firebird will use 87 octane.  They actually tell you not to use high octane. So what all this means is it depends on what your engine is designed for.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Tigger||10-24-2001||03:10 PM||||*sniff*  *sniff*<P>Awe man, who's using methane gas?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_watchout.gif"><P>89 octane runs ok for my duke. ||||reg||7
Z||000016||ray b||10-24-2001||03:15 PM||||octane numbers are vaired by adding MTB now instead of lead. MTB is a deadly poison way worse than the lead.<BR>REGULAR GAS MAKES MORE HP THAN HI-TEST it is more powerfull  with more BTU's per gal, hi-test is less exploseve and gives less power unless it knocks not mild ping but hard knocks lite pings on loaded motor is normal and will not cause problems.<BR>older cars with carbon in the heads may need hi-test so they don't ping but a new or clean rebuild do not need it.<BR>SAVE YOU $$$$ UNLESS IT IS CARBONED UP then clean it out.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000017||falconhulk||10-24-2001||03:38 PM||||Ray not sure about MTB, but they also use alcohol to change the octane rating today.....and its clean burning.<p>[This message has been edited by falconhulk (edited 10-24-2001).]||||reg||1
