Z||000026||artherd||10-25-2001||05:33 AM||||The 2.8 has what, an 8.5:1 compression ratio?  When new? when the bloody rings aren't leaking?<P>87 octane is what all my fieros have run best on, 89 or 92 gas is actually too HARD for the weak-ass stock ignition to even ignite. I get misfires. Bloody GM.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000027||FieroBUZZ||10-25-2001||06:48 AM||||I try to put 89 octane ethanol blend  in everytime but do use regular on occasion.<BR>Gary||||reg||1
Z||000028||Cozmo||10-25-2001||07:01 AM||||One word...<BR>Beano.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000029||Oreif||10-25-2001||08:18 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ray b:<BR><B>REGULAR GAS MAKES MORE HP THAN HI-TEST it is more powerfull  with more BTU's per gal, hi-test is less exploseve and gives less power unless it knocks not mild ping but hard knocks lite pings on loaded motor is normal and will not cause problems.<BR>older cars with carbon in the heads may need hi-test so they don't ping but a new or clean rebuild do not need it.<BR>SAVE YOU $$$$ UNLESS IT IS CARBONED UP then clean it out.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You are incorrect. Premium is  NOT "less explosive". It's flash point is higher. This causes the air/fuel mixture to burn in an even pattern. When the air/fuel burns unevenly or self-ignites, you have pinging. <BR>As the fuel burns evenly and completely you have smoother performance and lower emissions. ||||reg||1
Z||000030||mrtgman||10-25-2001||10:10 AM||||How about racing fuel? Anyone ever try that in thier fiero?<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fierofront1_1.jpg">   86 gt owned since new!<A HREF="http://www.palmbeachloan.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.palmbeachloan.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000031||Alex4mula||10-25-2001||10:48 AM||||Higher octane gas burns slower. At least in a new car if you don't adjust (advance) your timing you will loose performance (and $$) if you use higher oct than specified by manufacturer. I use 87 on my stock Formula and 93 in my 3.4 Formula (ign at 12°).<P>------------------<BR>    Alex4mula<BR>:) Red-Original Owner & White-1/94 with PM (:||||reg||1
